#!/home/node/opt/tgread-venv/bin/python3 -I
"""tgread: STRICTLY READ-ONLY local archive of the owner's Telegram chats (his own account, MTProto via Telethon).

Owner-approved. Data stays local. tgread never sends, edits, deletes, forwards, reacts, joins, leaves,
marks anything read, shows "typing" or sets the account online. Every MTProto request passes a 3-layer allowlist
guard (TelegramClient._call -> MTProtoSender.send -> MessagePacker, i.e. the wire); anything else raises
ReadOnlyViolation, is logged, and nothing is sent. `tgread allowlist` prints what is allowed.

Privacy (policy v2): message output that reaches the assistant is regex-masked (phones, emails,
IBAN and account numbers, cards, AHV, secret-bearing URLs, passwords/codes, coordinates), then anonymized by the
LOCAL LLM in one call (`llm-local --anonymize --owner-name ...`; summaries `--summary`), then masked again. llm-local
keeps names, addresses and facts and replaces intimate passages with "[private: <category>]" (the owner = "You");
since policy v3 also anyone's money/business details, the owner's own included ([private: money],
[private: business], figures -> [amount]).
With '--strict' in local_llm_anonymize_args / local_llm_summary_args (the old policy): Swiss addresses are masked
too, the LLM aliases names ("Person A/B", for that one output) and any known person name it missed becomes [NAME].
No local LLM -> regex masks incl. addresses + tgread's stable aliases (mapping only in the local DB) and a WARNING
line. LLM input above local_llm_max_chars is refused (narrow the request, or --llm-max-chars N and wait).
--raw prints exact text: ONLY when the owner explicitly asks for an exact quote.

Commands
  set-credentials                 api_id + api_hash on stdin -> ~/.secrets/tgread.json (0600); never echoed
  login-qr [--out PNG] [--timeout S] [--password-file F]
                                  QR login. Prints QR_READY / QR_REFRESHED path=..., PASSWORD_NEEDED, LOGIN_OK
  sync [--since D] [--chats SEL ...] [--quiet] [--max-seconds N]
  search QUERY [--chat SEL] [--from WHO] [--since D] [--until D] [-n N] [--context K] [--any] [--substring]
  recent [--chat SEL] [--from WHO] [-n N] [--hours H | --since D]
  unread-summary-data [--hours 24] [--include-muted] [--include-archived] [--include-read] [--per-chat N]
  summarize [--hours 24] [--chat SEL] [--unread]        (needs the local LLM)
  chats [--type T] [--grep S] [--all]
  exclude add SEL... | exclude rm SEL... | exclude list   (excluded chats are never fetched; stored messages deleted)
  purge SEL --yes                 delete the stored messages of one chat
  download --chat SEL --msg ID [--out DIR] [--max-mb N]  (explicit requests only)
  media --chat SEL [--msg ID] [--type T] [--since 30d] [-n 30] [--json]   files in a chat: metadata only
  alias set SEL NAME [--tag T] | alias rm NAME | alias list   my names for chats (accepted wherever --chat is)
  find-chat QUERY [--since 365d] [-n 10]   which chat? alias/tag, title, names, matching messages, recency
  who NAME                        a person's DM chat + the groups they posted in (counts, max 20)
  status [--check]  |  allowlist  |  wipe --session|--data|--all --yes
  live [--sync-minutes 15]        (the privacy vault keeps it running) ONE long-lived connection that receives the
                                  updates Telegram pushes (guard profile "live" = the read allowlist: still strictly
                                  read-only on the wire), stores new messages at once, runs the incremental sync every
                                  N minutes and queues new INCOMING messages for the vault's event feed (config
                                  live_events). Prints LIVE_READY / LIVE_QUEUED / SYNC_DONE lines.
  live-items [--after QID] [--max N] [--peek]   queued messages as JSON for the vault's event feed (regex-masked,
                                  real names: the vault triages and anonymizes them with its local LLM; never output)
  people-stats [--min N] [--days D]   DM contacts with >= N messages in D days as JSON: counts and dates only, no
                                  text (real names: the vault derives its person cards from it; never output)
  index-rows [--after-rowid N] [--max N] [--chat ID [--since-ts T]] [--chats]   DM/group messages as JSON for the
                                  vault's semantic index (raw text: the vault embeds it inside and keeps ids and
                                  numbers only; never output); --chats: only the chat ids the index may hold
  index-lines                     [[chat, msg], ...] on stdin -> search-style JSON hits of those messages (raw: the
                                  vault anonymizes them for its hybrid retrieval; never output)
  fts-query [--any]               JSON list of queries on stdin -> the FTS5 expressions of `search --expand`
  Message commands also take --sync (incremental sync first), --full (no shortening), --llm-max-chars N and
  --raw (see above).
Files: ~/.secrets/tgread.json + tgread.session (0600; tgread refuses to run if looser), ~/.config/tgread/config.json,
~/.local/share/tgread/ (0700: messages.db 0600 with FTS5, tgread.log, media/). Exit: 0 ok, 1 error, 2 usage,
3 not logged in, 4 guard blocked, 5 permissions, 6 local LLM missing, 75 try later. Tests: ~/opt/tgread-tests/run.sh.
Env (tests only): TGREAD_HOME, TGREAD_INBOX, TGREAD_SECRETSCAN, TGREAD_NOW, TGREAD_DEBUG. In the privacy vault
(claude-vault, its canonical home since 2026-10-10): TGREAD_FORCE_EXCLUDE=id,@user,... is always excluded.
